 "name": "Xuzhou Street Colour and Emotion: Five Scales (0–100)",
 "description": "Method replication: participants judge the colour environment of 20 street panoramas from inside Xuzhou's third ring road on five opposite-word scales from 0 to 100: boring–interesting, dangerous–safe, depressing–lively, poor–wealthy and ugly–beautiful. The images are not the paper's stimuli (43 Baidu street views fetched for this template, four directions at 90° field of view and 20° upward pitch joined into a 360° strip as in the paper, 2018–2022 captures).\n\nOriginal study: Hu, Xu, Wang, Wang, Li & Zhang (2023), \"Research on Street Color Environment Perception Based on CEP-KASS Framework\", Buildings 13(10), 2649. 30 volunteers familiar with the area scored at least 400 stitched Baidu panoramas each on the five 0–100 dimensions (adapted from the Place Pulse perceptions) in a human–machine adversarial scoring tool that suggested a score from the 50th image on; an SVM then predicted scores for about 27,000 points and the scores were related to K-means street colour clusters.\n\nDifferences from the original study: (1) 20 randomly drawn images per participant instead of 400 or more; (2) no model-suggested scores, every rating is the participant's own; (3) the colour clustering and score prediction are not part of the template.",
